ings is actually some kind of a  table in SAS and therefore if you know  how to access it you can grab it and  import it into your Mathematica notebook  so all of the results any analysis that  you can perform in SAS if it generates  any kind of a table of results and  that's pretty much all they do the only  exceptions are those procedures that  actually generate some kind of a PNG  file which is Sasa's way of providing a  plot except for those files as long as  it's a table of data you can import that  into mts using m2s link into your SAS  data set so in this particular example  I'm just going to use the m2 s submit  function to submit a little bit of code  this is SAS code here in the double  quotes and what this little chunk of  code says is I want you to create a  dataset called H H and that's all it  does and as a result when that happen  whenever you run em to us submit unless  you turn it off you'll get this results  viewer that basically tells you how do  things go and in this case there's no  output but there is this log at the  bottom that says ok everything worked  fine I created that data said it has 16  observations next I'm going to run this  program  which invokes proc mixed and what we're  going to do here is we're going to use  that HH data set that we just created  told SAS to create it SAS is holding on  to it we're now going to call m to s  submit again we're going to give it a  different snippet of SAS code and in  this case we're going to tell it to run  proc mixed using that data as input and  as output we want it to generate the  covariance a covariance matrix that  generate excuse me we're going to ask it  to generate a table that captures the  points in the likelihoods surface so  there's a covariance parameter estimate  that's generated in the final solution  and we're going to ask SAS to take that  solution and create a table that sort of  envelops that solution in a surface so  we can see what does the likelihood of  the residual log l